# Young adults (part d): facilitator scripts, tasks and witness stories.
R = {
'YA-9-1': dict(
  saint="Job: a righteous man who lost everything and kept asking God to explain.",
  tryit=["Write an honest lament.", "List what the Church says and does not say about suffering.", "Plan how to be present to someone in pain."],
  saintStory="Job lived in the land of Uz, a blameless and upright man who feared God, with seven sons and three daughters, and large flocks. One day, messengers came with news, one after another: raiders had taken his oxen and donkeys, fire from heaven had burned his sheep, enemies had seized his camels, and a great wind had collapsed the house where his children were feasting, killing them all. Job tore his robe, shaved his head and fell to the ground and worshiped: “Naked I came from my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return; the Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord.”\nThen he was struck with painful sores from head to foot. His wife said, “Curse God, and die.” Three friends came to comfort him. At first they sat with him in silence for seven days, and that was the best thing they did. Then they began to argue that he must have sinned. Job insisted he was innocent and demanded that God answer.\nGod did answer, out of the whirlwind, not with explanations but with questions about creation: “Where were you when I laid the foundation of the earth?” Job said, “I had heard of you by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees you.” God rebuked the friends and restored Job. The book doesn’t solve the problem of suffering, but shows a man who stays in conversation with God.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say gently, “Many of us have suffered, or watched someone suffer. Tonight we ask the question honestly: why does a good God allow suffering? You don’t have to share personal details.” Ask the group to write a single word describing what suffering has done to them or someone close. Collect them without comment.",
    teach=["Say: “This is the most serious objection to faith, and the Church does not pretend it is easy. The Catechism (CCC 309): ‘There is not a single aspect of the Christian message that is not in part an answer to the question of evil.’ The elements of the answer, taken together. One: God made the world good and did not create evil. Two: He gave creatures freedom, and love requires freedom; evil entered through the misuse of freedom (the fall). Many sufferings come from human sin: war, cruelty, injustice. Three: Some suffering comes from natural causes (disease, earthquakes), which the tradition links to a world not yet complete and wounded by the fall. Four: God permits evil but draws good from it, as Joseph told his brothers: ‘You meant evil against me; but God meant it for good.’ (Genesis 50:20)”", "Say: “Five: The most important: God has entered our suffering. In Jesus, God wept at the tomb of Lazarus, was hungry, was betrayed, was tortured and died. On the cross he cried, ‘My God, why have you forsaken me?’ We do not have a God who is distant from pain. Six: Suffering is not the last word. The Resurrection shows that evil and death are defeated; Revelation 21:4: ‘He will wipe away every tear.’ Seven: Suffering can be redemptive when united with Christ (Colossians 1:24). It does not mean God sends suffering to teach lessons, but that in his hands it can bear fruit: compassion, humility, trust, solidarity. The Church never says suffering is good; it says we must relieve it. Job: God does not explain, but he appears. Sometimes the answer to suffering is presence.”", "Say: “What not to say. ‘Everything happens for a reason.’ ‘God needed another angel.’ ‘It could have been worse.’ ‘You just need more faith.’ Job’s friends did harm by explaining. What helps: presence, listening, practical help, prayer, and honesty. The Psalms of lament teach us to pray our anger and grief to God. The problem of evil remains a mystery; the Church admits we don’t know all God’s reasons. Faith doesn’t take away the question; it gives a person to trust within it. For those suffering: it’s OK to be angry; keep praying; ask for help; counseling and medical care are good; don’t suffer alone. For others: show up, as the friends did in the first seven days. Josephine Bakhita, Maximilian Kolbe, Edith Stein and Mother Teresa found God in the darkness.”"],
    ask=["Did God create evil? (No; he created a good world and gave freedom.)", "What is the Christian answer at the center? (God entered our suffering in Jesus and defeated death.)", "What helps someone who suffers? (Presence, listening, practical help, prayer, honesty.)"],
    practice=["Read Psalm 13 or 22 aloud. Notice how it moves from complaint to trust.", "Write your own lament to God in a few honest lines, with no need to resolve it.", "In pairs, share how you want people to respond when you suffer.", "Choose one person who is suffering and plan one act of presence this week."],
    close="Pray Psalm 13 together and end with the Our Father. Sign of the Cross. If any member is in serious distress, offer to connect them with a priest or counselor after the session.")),

'YA-9-2': dict(
  saint="Georges Lemaître: a Belgian priest and physicist who proposed that the universe began in a “primeval atom.”",
  tryit=["Sort claims about science and faith.", "Mark real conflicts and myths.", "Write your own approach."],
  saintStory="Georges Lemaître was born in 1894 in Charleroi, Belgium. He served in the Belgian army in World War I, then studied mathematics and physics. He felt a call to the priesthood and was ordained in 1923, a Catholic priest who was also a professional scientist. He studied at Cambridge and at Harvard and MIT in the United States.\nIn 1927, he published a paper showing that Einstein’s equations implied an expanding universe, and he proposed that the recession of distant galaxies could be explained by it. Two years later Edwin Hubble’s observations supported it. In 1931 he suggested that if the universe is expanding, it must have started from a very small, dense state, which he called the “primeval atom,” or “cosmic egg.” This became the Big Bang theory, though the term came later, from a critic.\nIn 1951, Pope Pius XII gave an address saying that the Big Bang seemed to confirm the Genesis creation. Lemaître was uneasy: he believed that science and faith should not be mixed in that way, and he spoke to the pope’s staff, who got the pope to stop making such claims. Lemaître believed there were two paths to truth. He became president of the Pontifical Academy of Sciences in 1960, and died in 1966.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read two headlines, ‘Pope says evolution is real’ and ‘Scientists confirm universe had a beginning.’ Ask the group which they had heard, and what they thought it meant. List the reactions.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that faith and reason can’t be in real conflict because God is the author of both (CCC 159). John Paul II: ‘Science can purify religion from error and superstition; religion can purify science from idolatry and false absolutes.’ Science asks how and what, using observation and experiment. Faith asks why and who, using revelation and reason. Each has its own methods. Problems arise when one oversteps: scientism (the idea that only what science can measure is real) is a philosophy, not a scientific result; and a literalism that reads Genesis as a science textbook misreads a text whose purpose is theological.”", "Say: “Key issues. Creation and the Big Bang: the Church teaches creation out of nothing and God’s continual sustaining of the universe. This is a metaphysical claim about dependence, not a cosmology. The Big Bang is a scientific theory of the universe’s expansion from a hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago; it fits creation but doesn’t prove it. Evolution: the Church doesn’t oppose evolution as a scientific theory. Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) allowed that the human body may have evolved; John Paul II (1996) said evolution is ‘more than a hypothesis’; but each human soul is directly created by God, and humans are not accidents. The Church rejects materialist ideologies that deny purpose. Adam and Eve: the Catechism affirms the original human couple and the fall as ‘a primeval event’ in history, while recognizing symbolic language in Genesis 3 (CCC 390). How to reconcile this with science is debated.”", "Say: “Myths and cases. Galileo (1633): a real mistake in prudential judgment about interpreting Scripture and weighing evidence; the pope and Church were not against science; he was not tortured or burned; many Church figures were supporters. The ‘warfare thesis’ (Draper, White, 19th century) is rejected by historians of science. The Church patronized science: universities, Copernicus (a cleric), Mendel (friar), Lemaître (priest), Steno, Secchi. The Vatican Observatory is one of the oldest. Today many scientists are believers. Bioethics raises new issues. Approach: be curious and humble; study both; don’t be defensive; separate scientific claims from philosophical ones; keep the theological meaning of Genesis (creation is good, God is the source, humans are made in God’s image, there is a purpose). Lemaître’s approach: do real science and don’t use it as apologetics. Psalm 19:1: ‘The heavens declare the glory of God.’”"],
    ask=["Can faith and science truly conflict? (No; the same God is author of both.)", "What does the Church say about evolution? (The body may have evolved; each soul is directly created.)", "What was Lemaître’s concern with Pius XII’s 1951 address? (He wanted to keep science and theology from being confused.)"],
    practice=["Sort statements into myth, partly true, and real question: ‘The Church burned scientists,’ ‘Genesis is a science text,’ ‘Evolution disproves God,’ ‘Science proves there is no soul.’", "Choose one real question and write how a thoughtful Catholic might approach it.", "Look up one Catholic scientist and share one fact.", "Write your own guideline for discussing faith and science with a friend."],
    close="Pray Psalm 8 or 19. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read a short article from the Vatican Observatory or a book by a Catholic scientist.")),

'YA-9-3': dict(
  saint="Francis of Assisi at Damietta: a friar who crossed a battle line in 1219 to speak to the Sultan.",
  tryit=["Write what we share with Jews, Muslims and other Christians.", "Write where we truly differ.", "Plan a respectful conversation."],
  saintStory="In 1219, during the Fifth Crusade, Christian armies besieged the Egyptian city of Damietta. Francis of Assisi had come to the camp, troubled by the violence on both sides. He asked permission to go and speak to the Sultan, al-Malik al-Kamil, a Muslim ruler. The papal legate reluctantly agreed. Francis, with one companion, Brother Illuminatus, crossed the lines unarmed.\nThey were seized by the Sultan’s soldiers, beaten and chained, and brought before al-Kamil. The Sultan, a man of culture and courtesy, received them and listened to Francis preach the Gospel. Medieval accounts say Francis offered to walk through fire to show the truth of Christ, which the Sultan’s scholars refused. The Sultan was impressed by Francis’s sincerity. He offered gifts, which Francis declined, and allowed him to return safely to the Christian camp.\nWe don’t know exactly what was said. But Francis’s approach became a model. In his Rule of 1221, he wrote that friars who go among Muslims may live peacefully, without arguing, “subject to every human creature for God’s sake,” and, when it pleases God, announce the Word. In 2019, Pope Francis marked the 800th anniversary of the meeting by signing a Document on Human Fraternity with the Grand Imam of al-Azhar in Abu Dhabi.",
  script=dict(
    open="Play a short audio clip of a call to prayer, then a Jewish Shema or a Gregorian chant. Ask, “What do you hear? What do you feel? What do these have in common?” Take reactions.",
    teach=["Say: “Vatican II’s Nostra Aetate (1965) is the foundation. It begins by looking at what humans share: the questions about the meaning of life. It says: ‘The Catholic Church rejects nothing that is true and holy in these religions. She regards with sincere reverence those ways of conduct and of life, those precepts and teachings which, though differing in many particulars from what she holds and sets forth, nonetheless often reflect a ray of that Truth which enlightens all men.’ At the same time, the Church ‘proclaims, and is duty bound to proclaim without fail, Christ, who is ‘the way, the truth, and the life’ (John 14:6).’ So dialogue and proclamation go together. Dialogue is not relativism; it’s listening with respect while holding our convictions.”", "Say: “Judaism. Our roots: the Old Testament is our Scripture; Jesus, Mary, the apostles were Jews. Paul: ‘The gifts and the call of God are irrevocable.’ (Romans 11:29) The Church condemns antisemitism and recognizes that the Jewish people are not guilty of Jesus’ death. John Paul II called Jews our ‘elder brothers.’ We differ on Jesus as Messiah. Islam: Muslims worship the one God, honor Jesus as prophet and Mary as virgin mother, pray, fast and give alms. We differ on the Trinity, the Incarnation, the crucifixion, and the status of the Qur’an and Muhammad. Hinduism and Buddhism: they seek answers about suffering, liberation; the Church looks for ‘seeds of the Word’; we differ on God, the person, salvation. Other Christians: Orthodox (very close; differ on the papacy), Protestants (differ on authority, sacraments, Mary). All baptized are brothers and sisters in Christ.”", "Say: “How to engage. The Church describes four forms of dialogue: of life (living as good neighbors), of action (working together for justice), of theological exchange (scholars), and of religious experience (sharing prayer and spirituality, carefully). Practical: learn from sources they would recognize; ask rather than assume; listen to their story; share your own; don’t caricature; avoid arguing to win; know your faith; pray for them. Avoid indifferentism (all religions the same) and syncretism (mixing). Avoid arrogance and also timidity. Francis of Assisi neither compromised nor attacked. Evangelization: respects freedom; invites by witness. The Church teaches that salvation is possible for those who sincerely seek God (LG 16). Why are we still Catholic? Because we believe the fullness of truth is in Christ and his Church, and this fullness is a gift. Charity and truth together.”"],
    ask=["What does Nostra Aetate say about other religions? (The Church rejects nothing true and holy in them.)", "How does the Church view the Jewish people? (As elder brothers; the covenant is irrevocable; antisemitism is condemned.)", "What is the difference between dialogue and syncretism? (Dialogue listens and respects; syncretism mixes beliefs.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart with Judaism, Islam, Hinduism/Buddhism, Orthodoxy and Protestantism: what we share, where we differ, how to engage.", "Pick one friend or neighbor of another faith and plan a respectful question to ask.", "Write a short statement of what you would want a friend to know about your faith.", "Pray for them by name."],
    close="Pray the Prayer of Saint Francis: “Lord, make me an instrument of your peace.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to visit a different house of worship by invitation.")),

'YA-10-1': dict(
  saint="Pier Giorgio Frassati and the Society of the Shady Characters: friends who climbed mountains and prayed the Rosary on the way.",
  tryit=["List the friends who help you grow.", "Name one place of accountability.", "Plan one act of community this month."],
  saintStory="Pier Giorgio Frassati was born in 1901 in Turin, Italy, to a wealthy family. His father owned the newspaper La Stampa and was an ambassador. Pier Giorgio was a handsome, athletic young man who loved skiing, climbing, and practical jokes. He was also deeply devout, going to Mass daily, praying the Rosary and adoring the Eucharist. He took part in Catholic student groups and served the poor with the St. Vincent de Paul Society, often giving away his money and even his coat.\nHe gathered a group of friends, who called themselves the Società dei Tipi Loschi, the “Society of Shady Characters.” They played pranks, took mountain trips, ate together and prayed together. They sang, climbed peaks, and said the Rosary on the trail. His motto was Verso l’alto, “To the heights.” The friendships lasted beyond his life, and the friends supported each other in faith.\nHe contracted polio, probably from the sick he tended, and died at twenty-four in 1925. His funeral was filled with the poor of Turin, whom his family had not known he helped. He was beatified in 1990, and canonized in 2025. His friends wrote letters about him for years.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “Who are the three people you spend the most time with? What do they bring out in you? Do they help you grow in faith or pull away from it?” Allow silence, then take a few answers.",
    teach=["Say: “We are made for communion. ‘It is not good that the man should be alone.’ (Genesis 2:18) Christianity is not a private matter. Jesus formed a community: the Twelve, and a larger circle of disciples. The early Church ‘devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers.’ (Acts 2:42) The Catechism speaks of the Church as communion. Friendship is a gift and a path to holiness. Aelred of Rievaulx wrote Spiritual Friendship; Aquinas said friendship is the highest form of love; Jesus calls us friends (John 15:15). A friend shares your journey and helps you toward God. Proverbs 27:17: ‘Iron sharpens iron, and one man sharpens another.’”", "Say: “Community. Where do we find it? The parish: regular Mass, small groups, service. Campus ministry, young adult groups, Bible studies, prayer groups, retreats, sports leagues and service projects. Movements: Catholic Charismatic Renewal, Focolare, Communion and Liberation, FOCUS, Theology on Tap. Families. Small groups matter because we need people who know us. In our culture, loneliness is rising. Social media gives connections but not always community. What makes community healthy: shared purpose, regular meetings, honesty, hospitality, forgiveness, mutual care, openness to newcomers. What harms it: gossip, cliques, judgment, perfectionism, abuse of authority. If a Church community has hurt you, seek another, and speak with leaders.”", "Say: “Accountability. Hebrews 10:24-25: ‘Let us consider how to stir up one another to love and good works, not neglecting to meet together.’ James 5:16: ‘Confess your sins to one another and pray for one another, that you may be healed.’ Accountability means having people who ask hard questions out of love: Are you praying? Are you going to Mass? How are you living chastity? How are you using money? It works best when mutual, voluntary, specific, and gentle. Form a small group of two to four people who meet weekly or monthly, with a simple pattern: check-in, Scripture, prayer, commitments. Be careful about the difference between accountability and control. A spiritual director or a confessor is another form of accountability. Pier Giorgio’s friends: they stayed in each other’s lives. Choose friends who raise you toward the heights.”"],
    ask=["Why is Christianity communal? (Jesus formed a community; we are made for communion.)", "Name two forms of community. (Parish, small groups, movements, campus ministry.)", "What makes accountability healthy? (Mutual, voluntary, specific, gentle, rooted in love.)"],
    practice=["Draw a circle of your relationships: closest friends, mentors, community. Mark who helps you toward God.", "Identify one gap: a mentor, a group, an accountability partner.", "Write an invitation to someone to start a weekly or monthly check-in.", "Plan one community event for the coming month."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give us friends who lead us to you.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to make a plan for a regular small group.")),

'YA-10-2': dict(
  saint="The Samaritan woman: she left her water jar at the well and told her whole town.",
  tryit=["Write your story in three parts.", "Practice telling it for two minutes.", "Name one person you will invite."],
  saintStory="In John 4, Jesus, tired from his journey, sat down by Jacob’s well in Samaria at noon. A Samaritan woman came to draw water, probably at noon to avoid the other women. Jews and Samaritans did not associate. Jesus asked her, “Give me a drink.” She was surprised. Jesus spoke of “living water.” She asked for it. Then he said, “Go, call your husband.” She said she had none. Jesus said, “You are right… you have had five husbands, and he whom you now have is not your husband.” She said, “Sir, I perceive that you are a prophet.”\nShe raised a theological question about where to worship, and Jesus said, “The hour is coming, and now is, when the true worshipers will worship the Father in spirit and truth.” She said, “I know that Messiah is coming.” Jesus answered, “I who speak to you am he.”\nThe woman left her water jar and went into the town, saying, “Come, see a man who told me all that I ever did. Can this be the Christ?” Many Samaritans believed because of her testimony, and then they asked Jesus to stay two days. They said, “It is no longer because of your words that we believe, for we have heard for ourselves.” In Orthodox tradition, she is called Photina, “the enlightened one,” and honored as an evangelist.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand out cards and ask each person to write the last time someone invited them to something that changed their life. Share a few stories. Say, “That is how most conversions begin: with an invitation.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Great Commission (Matthew 28:19-20) applies to every baptized person. Pope Francis in Evangelii Gaudium: ‘Every Christian is a missionary to the extent that he or she has encountered the love of God in Christ Jesus.’ (EG 120) Evangelization is the Church’s deepest identity (Paul VI). It has many forms: witness of life, proclamation, invitation, service, and sharing community. The new evangelization, as John Paul II said, is new in ardor, methods and expression. Most people come to faith through relationships. The Samaritan woman didn’t have a theology degree. She told her story and invited others to ‘come and see.’ Philip said the same to Nathanael.”", "Say: “How to share. Start by listening: Jesus asked for a drink, then listened. Share your story: a simple three-part testimony: my life before, how I encountered Christ, and how my life is now. Be honest, including struggles. Invite: to Mass, a Bible study, a retreat, an event. Pray for the person first. Be ready to answer: 1 Peter 3:15: ‘Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence.’ Know the basics: the kerygma (God loves you, Jesus died and rose for your sins, you are invited to follow him), and a few apologetics answers. Don’t argue to win. Live the faith with joy and integrity. Serve. Use social media wisely: positive, charitable. Respect freedom: the Spirit converts.”", "Say: “Common difficulties. Fear of rejection: Jesus said the world would reject us, but we are sent anyway. Fear of ignorance: say ‘I don’t know, but I’ll find out.’ Wanting to be liked: the Gospel will sometimes be countercultural. Sharing with family: patience; witness first. Sharing with friends: invite them to join a group. A culture of ‘nones’ (the religiously unaffiliated): many are open but hurt. Evangelize those who left: listen to their reasons. Evangelization and service go together: Catholic Relief Services, soup kitchens. Pray: ‘Lord, send me.’ The woman left her jar: she dropped her ordinary task for the sake of the news. What jar do you need to put down? Francis Xavier traveled the world; you may be called to your dorm hall or your workplace.”"],
    ask=["Who is called to evangelize? (Every baptized Christian.)", "What are the three parts of a testimony? (Before, encounter with Christ, now.)", "What did the Samaritan woman say? (“Come, see a man who told me all that I ever did.”)"],
    practice=["Read John 4:5-30, 39-42 aloud in parts.", "Write your faith story in three short paragraphs: before, encounter, now.", "Practice telling it to a partner in two minutes; the partner gives encouragement and one question.", "Name one person you will invite to something this month, and pray for them by name."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, send me, and give me words and love.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to share their story with one person this week.")),

'YA-10-3': dict(
  saint="Pachomius: an Egyptian soldier who wrote the first rule for monks living together.",
  tryit=["Draft your rule of life.", "Include prayer, sacraments, study, service, rest and relationships.", "Choose a first month trial."],
  saintStory="Pachomius was born around 292 in Upper Egypt to pagan parents. At twenty, he was conscripted into the Roman army. While his unit was being taken down the Nile, the soldiers were locked up in a prison in Thebes. Local Christians brought them food and water, kindness that astonished Pachomius. He asked who these people were, and was told they were Christians, who loved all humans. He vowed that if he was released, he would become a Christian and serve people.\nAfter his discharge, he was baptized and went to live with an elderly hermit named Palamon. But then he felt that God was calling him to something else: a community. Around 320 he founded a monastery at Tabennese, on the Nile. Monks lived together, with a common rule: a time for prayer, work, meals, silence, and obedience to an abbot. Before this, hermits had lived alone. Pachomius organized them in a system of communities, eventually thousands of monks, and also communities of women.\nHis rule was translated by Jerome into Latin and influenced Basil, Benedict and others. He died in 348 in a plague. His idea: ordinary people, in community, with a rule, can live the Gospel.",
  script=dict(
    open="Display a weekly calendar template. Ask everyone to fill in one typical week quickly, with colors for work, rest, relationships and prayer. Compare patterns. Say, “A rule of life begins by seeing the week as it is.”",
    teach=["Say: “A rule of life is a set of practices that you commit to, to keep God at the center. The word ‘rule’ comes from the Latin regula, a pattern or trellis, not a cage. A trellis supports growth. The great rules: St. Pachomius, St. Basil, St. Augustine, St. Benedict (the most influential, with ‘pray and work,’ stability, obedience), St. Francis, St. Ignatius. Lay people can have a rule, too: St. Francis de Sales’s Introduction to the Devout Life; the Third Orders; Opus Dei’s ‘plan of life.’ Jesus himself had a rhythm: he withdrew to pray, attended the synagogue, observed feasts, spent time with friends and the crowds.”", "Say: “Elements. Prayer: daily (morning offering, a time of Scripture or silence, evening examination), Angelus or Rosary. Sacraments: Mass every Sunday, perhaps weekdays; Confession monthly. Study: spiritual reading, Scripture, the Catechism. Service: regular works of mercy. Community: small group, family, parish. Rest: Sabbath, sleep, leisure. Sacrifice: fasting on Fridays, giving. Work: done well. Relationships: time for family and friends. Guidance: a spiritual director. Principles: Be realistic (start small); be specific (what, when, where); be flexible but faithful; include rest; review regularly with someone. A rule for a student differs from that of a young professional or a parent. Some start with a simple Fruits-of-the-Spirit checklist.”", "Say: “How to make one. Step 1: Reflect on your life now. Where does time go? Step 2: Name your goals. What do I want my life to look like in a year? Step 3: Choose a few key practices. Four or five. Example: Daily: ten minutes of prayer with Scripture; evening Examen. Weekly: Sunday Mass, one holy hour or weekday Mass, a small group, a work of mercy. Monthly: Confession, a day of reflection. Yearly: a retreat. Step 4: Tell someone. Step 5: Try it for a month, then adjust. Step 6: Be gentle with failure; start again. Pope John XXIII’s spiritual journal, Journal of a Soul, shows a lifetime of such rules. The rule is a servant, not a master. As Benedict wrote, we run the path of God’s commandments with hearts overflowing with the inexpressible sweetness of love. The Church’s liturgical year provides a rhythm too. Christ calls disciples to follow him daily (Luke 9:23).”"],
    ask=["What is a rule of life? (A set of practices that keeps God at the center and supports growth.)", "Name three elements of a rule. (Prayer, sacraments, study, service, community, rest.)", "How long should you try it before adjusting? (About a month.)"],
    practice=["Review how your week actually goes: where does time go?", "Draft a rule of life with four or five practices: daily, weekly, monthly, yearly.", "Share it with a partner who will check in at the end of the month.", "Put the first practice on your calendar."],
    close="Pray the Suscipe of Benedict, “Receive me, Lord, according to your word.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to review their rules together in a month.")),
}
